Ah, YANIs, bane of my free time.

> 	Starvation.  Gehennom is the spirit world, not the real 
> world.  No one has any reason to leave food lying around.  Also, 
> when the spirit of a creature is 'killed', there's no corpse:
> "the body of the red dragon slowly fades away".  

Perhaps food eaten in Gehennom tasting "like dust" would
get the point across? And it having only a percentage of
its normal nutritive value (if not blessed). Perhaps the normal
rate of recovery of HP and MP could also be reduced (or
zeroed (!)) in Gehennom (and !oGE restores a lot more MP).
Or maybe this would increase the feeling of "Oh gosh this
SUCKS" beyond all endurance. 

> 	Anti-magic traps.  These now only work on people without magic
> resistance.  In the early game, this boils down to "Don't hurt him!
> He's a wizard, and he needs his spell points!"  In the late game, this
> boils down to "no effect".

I've for awhile liked the idea that most traps should scale their
nastiness with the dungeon level they appear on. Arrow traps
shoot multiple arrows with more force, more often poisoned.
Bear traps give electric shocks, cause nasty leg damage; pits
are deeper; rock traps drop boulders; rust traps become
disenchanter traps...

> 	Enchanting magic resistant items.  (Jakob Creutzig)  Items
> giving MR resist being enchanted 80% of the time.  

Or maybe this would encourage tedious strategies (just throw
more scrolls at it). Why not make it 100% and have the items
always be +0?

Likewise, perhaps have SDSM and [SoR relfect the spells
back on some random inventory item sometimes, the other
times the enchantment takes, getting trapped in the reflection?

I've liked for awhile balancing out [SG]DSM by lowering their
AC benefit; this idea is similar and (IMHO) more justifiable
and fun.

> 	Special case:  gray dragon scales only resist 60% of the 
> time, and you're told "the GDS glows, then resists being enchanted!"
> The message keeps new players from giving up on all dragon scales as
> worthless.

With a suitable message this shouldn't be a problem...

> 	Blessed items.  Where blessed items now do the same thing
> as unblessed items, make them just a little bit better.  Not enough
> to have any significant gameplay effect, but enough to make spoiled
> adventurers eagerly bless everything in sight (and possibly run low
> on holy water).

This is okay but can only be as good as the specific list of
changes. To balance, should new cursing effects be created?
A cursed wand of teleport might teleport only itself (the wand)
away.

> 	Speed stacks.  If you have speed boots or the speed intrinsic
> or you quaffed a potion of speed, your speed goes up by one step.  
> You need two at the same time to get to very fast.

Perhaps the wand of speed would give temporary very fast,
and intrinsic speed would be obtained by mixing speed sources
(a la eating a stalker corpse while temporarily invisible). This
would make the wand more interesting, but this is more of
an early/middle game challenge than anything late game...

> 	The speed intrinsic times out.  If testing shows that 5000
